3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Georges E. Seligmann, a Manhattan dealer who sold works of art to major museums around the world, died on Tuesday at his home in Manhattan. He was 102.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Seligmann, who was born and reared in Paris, was trained as a doctor. But just as he was about to set up his own practice, he changed his mind and joined his family's art gallery. His uncles, Jacques and Arnold Seligmann, with
2 CONC  his father, Simon, founded a gallery in Paris in 1879. As the business grew, the Seligmann brothers opened branches in London and New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 1930's, Dr. Seligmann helped the Louvre acquire David's ''Portrait of Napoleon Bonaparte, First Consul,'' the only painting Napoleon ever sat for. He also sold one of five remaining Ingres portraits, ''Madame Moitessier, Se
2 CONC ated,'' to the National Gallery in London.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940, facing the German invasion of France, Dr. Seligmann moved to New York and joined the Jacques Seligmann Gallery on East 57th Street in Manhattan, which handled a range of art from 18th-century French works to Impressionist
2 CONC  and modern paintings. Among the gallery's clients were J. P. Morgan, George Blumenthal, William Randolph Hearst, Lord Rothermere and Sir William Burrell.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1946, Dr. Seligmann married Edna Horn Mandel of Manhattan. In 1949, he opened his own dealership, which prospered until his retirement in 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Seligmann's father collected rare medieval works and left them to his son, who in turn gave two rare enamels to the Metropolitan Museum of Art.
