2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Leopold Rosenheim (1835-1889)
2 CONT
2 CONT by Evelyn Wilcock
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Leopold Rosenheim was born on 25 February 1835 in Heidingsfeld, Bavaria, home to the many Jews who were excluded from the neighbouring Bishopric city of Würzburg. It is said that Joseph wanted to be a doctor but was prevent
2 CONC ed by anti-Semitic laws. The family believed he had instead worked as a bookseller in Prague. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph was a younger son of a wine merchant and cigar manufacturer and it was to expand this business selling the then fashionable wine from Franconia that he came to London in 1857 when he was 22. Though Joseph was the first of h
2 CONC is family to arrive in the UK, he was soon followed by his more businesslike younger brother Hermann. Together they founded L. Rosenheim and Sons, Wine Merchants. Joseph was naturalised on 1 Jan 1863 and the following year he marr
2 CONC ied Johanna Heim, daughter of Felix Heim a notable banker in Würzburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph moved to Liverpool in 1872 or 1873. The family story is that he was dispatched there to expand the wine business in the north of England. Another cousin who lived in Britain moved to Bordeaux. However, once in Liverpool, Jo
2 CONC seph became a founder of Lehman, Neugass, Rosenheim & Sons, Cotton Brokers, a change of direction probably spurred by the Rosenheim connection to Lehmann Brothers who had built their fortune trading cotton in Alabama.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rosenheims were comfortably off. The 1881 census shows the family at 1 Croxteth Road. They had 4 British servants and a German nurse. Their ten children regularly exchanged visits with their German relatives in Würzburg and Fr
2 CONC ankfurt, and some married children of wealthy Liverpool congregants. Many of his Rosenheim cousins pursued successful professional careers in Britain. But Joseph seems to have remained an outsider. His family appear to have been R
2 CONC eform Jews in Germany and Hermann was associated with the West London Reform Synagogue in London. In Liverpool there was no alternative to the orthodox synagogues in which Joseph was ill at ease. He died of cancer on 19 January 18
2 CONC 89 in his early fifties and the cotton business was continued by Johanna and her sons after her husband died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grave Reference
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Leopold Rosenheim (1835-1889): B 12.03
2 CONT
