1 NAME David Tevele /Schiff/ Chief Rabbi of the United Kingdom
2 GIVN David Tevele
2 NSFX Chief Rabbi of the United Kingdom
2 _MARNM Schiff
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1722
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 DEC 1791
2 PLAC London, England
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE 20 DEC 1791
2 PLAC Alderney Road (Globe Rd) Jewish Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 ADR1 27 Alderney Road
3 CITY London
3 STAE England
3 POST E1 4EG
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 OCCU Chief Rabbi of London
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tevele_Schiff
2 CONT
2 CONT Chief Rabbi David Tevele Schiff (Hebrew: דוד טעבלי שיף‎; died December 17, 1791) was the chief rabbi of the United Kingdom and the rabbi of the Great Synagogue of London from 1765 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was son of Solomon Schiff, member of a famous and learned family from Frankfurt am Main. His mother was Roesche, daughter of the quarrelsome Reb Aberle London. Tevele Schiff was educated in the schools of Rabbis Jacob Poper and
2 CONC  Jacob Joshua Falk. He served as maggid in Vienna. He also was head of the Beth Midrash in Worms, and later dayan in Frankfurt.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Hart Lyon left London in 1764 it was agreed that his successor should be appointed and maintained by the Great Synagogue and the Hambro' Synagogue jointly. However, they could not agree on a single name. The Great Synagogue 
2 CONC appointed Rabbi Schiff on February 24, 1765, while the Hambro' Synagogue appointed Rabbi Israel Meshullam Zalman (who became known in England as Meshullam Solomon).
2 CONT
2 CONT Each Rabbi claimed authority over the whole Kingdom. The problem was resolved only after a split within the community at Portsmouth. Although a dissident group of the Portsmouth Jewish population established a rival congregation r
2 CONC ecognising the authority of Meshullam Solomon, the main community formally accepted the authority of Rabbi Schiff. When in 1766 the Rabbi of the Great Synagogue began to be known by the title "The Chief Rabbi", and Meshullam Solom
2 CONC on returned to Hamburg in 1780, all communities accepted Tevele Schiff's authority.
2 CONT
2 CONT Schiff carried on a detailed correspondence with his brother the Rabbi of Copenhagen, which casts an interesting light on Jewish social life in Georgian London. The economic depression following the end of the American War of Inde
2 CONC pendence led to frequent calls for the reduction of his salary. In addition to this, after Rabbi Meshullam Solomon left London, Rabbi Schiff was expected to do the work of the Hambro' Synagogue as well. Overworked and underpaid, h
2 CONC e tried unsuccessfully to obtain appointments at Rotterdam and Würzburg in 1781 and 1782 respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on December 17, 1791, after having occupied the Rabbinate for twenty-seven years. His wife, Breinle Sinzheim, had died many years before, shortly after his appointment to London, leaving him an only son, Moses. He was in t
2 CONC he old burial-ground in Mile End, in immediate proximity to Rabbi Aaron Hart and the founders of the congregation. His son, Moses Schiff, perpetuated his memory by arranging for the publication of his responsa, sermons, and exposi
2 CONC tions, under the title Lashon Zahav, which means the "Golden tongue".
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT Alderman, Geoffrey (1998). Modern British Jewry. Oxford University Press. ISBN 0-1982-0759-X.
2 CONT
2 CONT Taylor, Derek (2007). British chief rabbis, 1664-2006. Vallentine Mitchell. ISBN 0-8530-3610-1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shmuel, Feiner; Chaya Naor (2003). The Jewish Enlightenment. University of Pennsylvania Press. ISBN 0-8122-3755-2.
2 CONT
2 CONT Endelman, Todd M. (2002). The Jews of Britain, 1656 to 2000. University of California Press. ISBN 0-5202-2720-4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Katz, David S. (1997). The Jews in the History of England, 1485-1850. Oxford University Press. ISBN 0-1982-0667-4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Past Chief Rabbis
2 CONT
2 CONT The History of the Great Synagogue
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Chief Rabbi David Tevele Schiff (Hebrew: דוד טעבלי שיף‎) (died December 17, 1791; or, in the Hebrew calendar, 26 Kislev 5551) was the chief rabbi of Great Britain and the rabbi of the Great Synagogue of London from 1765 until his 
2 CONC death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Schiff was a disciple of Rabbi Joshua Falk, author of the Classic Commentary on the Talmud "Pene Yehoshua". He was a contemporary of Rabbi Yechezkel Sega"l Landau, Prague's Chief Rabbi and author of the fundamental Responsae
2 CONC  "Noda B'Yhuda". His most famous disciple was the holy Rabbi Nosson Adler of Frankfurt-am-Main, famous for his Kabbalistic teachings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Schiff's resting place is at Britain's first Ashkenazic cemetery since the expulsion of Jewry in medieval times. It is situated at 27 Alderney Road, London E1 4EG, in London's East End.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
