1 NAME Freudche /Goldschmied-Hameln/
2 GIVN Freudche
2 SURN Goldschmied-Hameln
2 _MARNM Nathan
2 NICK Fradche
1 SEX F
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 APR 1770
2 PLAC Schwabach, Bavaria
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dear Randy,
2 CONT
2 CONT Here are two fascinating tweaks, both connected to Glueckl.  The first is traceable to a statement made by David Kaufmann in his introduction (in German) to one of the Yiddish editions of her book (see attached clip), made because
2 CONC  he had seen a dedication in the book Toldot Avraham which showed that the wife of the Hoffaktor Elia Beer (Elias Baer Nathan) in Schwabach was Freudchen bt Josef Goldschmidt Hameln. When he looked into it, he apparently was told 
2 CONC that this lady was named after the grandmother, Freudchen Spanier, and, from there, he apparently assumed that the Josef in question was Glueckl’s son, Josef, who moved to Kopenhagen.  None of the Danish sources acknowledge her, a
2 CONC nd someone on My Ancestry (and Geni) has found her death date (perhaps from a gravestone) in Schwabach, which should be helpful in finding more about her. See attached clips for her and Josef.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Ettlinger unknowingly found the key to the whole thing:   image001.jpg  Rechle’s father is the same Elia Beer Schwabach (he is called “stadlan” in her gravestone) and her grandfather is not Josef bn Chaim & Glueckl but 
2 CONC Josef bn Moses bn Isaac of Frankfurt.  And, of course, Freudchen would still be the namesake of Freudchen Spanier, because she is her descendant too.
2 CONT
2 CONT Here is Fradche/Freudchan’s Geni screen. The two other girls, Roeschen and Esther, really do belong to Josef of Kopenhagen, and it’s only Fradche who is a problem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy High
