1 NAME Amschel Anchel /Oppenheim/
2 GIVN Amschel Anchel
2 SURN Oppenheim
2 _MARNM Worms
2 NICK Amschel Anchel Gershon Worms
2 NICK Worms-Oppenheim
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 MAR 1635
2 ADDR
3 CITY Frankfurt
3 STAE Hesse
3 CTRY Germany
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} zum Fuchs
2 CONT
2 CONT (From Nancy High) Amschel Worms Oppenheim  https://www.geni.com/people/Amschel-Worms/6000000007801718322  was the brother of Jacob Oppenheim of Worms, who died in Frankfurt 26 Feb 1638 (copy of Ele Toldot sheet attached). The Beer
2 CONC digungsbuch (clip attached) confirms that he was buried next to Amschel, his brother, and Ettlinger agrees that he is this Amschel (clip attached).  Unfortunately, at some point Ettlinger listed Wolf Oppenheim who died in 1623 as 
2 CONC Amschel’s father, not noticing that he could not be Jacob’s father (nor Amschel’s).
2 CONT
2 CONT Amschel is shown in several of the records extracted by Andernacht, including one from early 1604 (clip attached), when he was already living in the Fuchs, apparently having come there from Wertheim (see below).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob is on page 14 of the Worms Memorbuch as “Rabbi Jacob bn morenu Rav Simon Oppenheim, died in Frankfurt at Purim 1638”. He appears to be Jacob Oppenheim of the house of the Trumm in Worms, who was already raising his family in
2 CONC  1610. They also have a brother Mendel of the house of the roten Loewen in Worms, who was a Parnas and never left there, dying in Worms in 1635 after losing a son, Wolf (see his Ele Toldot sheet, attached), to the same Frankfurt 1
2 CONC 632 plague which killed Amschel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Going back to Amschel for the purpose of disconnecting him from being son of the Wolf Oppenheim who died 12 Jan 1623:  Amschel’s son, Wolf, is marrying or becoming engaged in 1620 and at that time, Amschel claims that Wolf was bor
2 CONC n in Frankfurt, when, in fact, Wolf was born in Wertheim (see attached clip from Fuchs house page), where Amschel was living at that time of Wolf’s birth (perhaps this was the home town of Amschel’s wife). So, given the strictness
2 CONC  of the naming patterns of the time, it is out of the question that Amschel would have named his son after his father if his father did not die until 1623.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just as an added note, there are two Simon Wolf Oppenheims in Worms who are not the father of these three men, but the time period in which their father needs to have died is just too early for the Kobets al Jad (Worms Memorbuch) 
2 CONC and for the house lists, which begin in 1605. He will, however, show up one way or another in the Gruene Buch, and going through that more thoroughly will surely reveal him before long.
2 CONT
