1 NAME Walter Nathan /Rothschild/ Jr.
2 GIVN Walter Nathan
2 NSFX Jr.
2 _MARNM Rothschild
1 NAME Bill /Rothschild/
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 FEB 1920
2 PLAC New York, New York
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 20 JUN 2003
2 PLAC Huntington, Long Island, New York
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Walter N. Rothschild Jr., who stepped down as president of the Abraham & Straus department store chain to pursue a civic career, died Friday at Huntington Hospital on Long Island. He was 83.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cause was complications from surgery, his son, Trip, said.
2 CONT
2 CONT After spending nine years as president of Abraham & Straus, Mr. Rothschild's decision to leave the corporate world in 1969 surprised his colleagues and generated a front-page story in The New York Times.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Rothschild, the great-grandson of a founder of Abraham & Straus, Abraham Abraham, represented the fourth generation of his family to serve as the company's president.
2 CONT
2 CONT The chain, based in Brooklyn and widely known as A. & S., once comprised 18 department stores across the New York metropolitan region, but the name disappeared in 1995, when its parent company, Federated Department Stores Inc., co
2 CONC nverted the stores into branches of other chains like Macy's or Stern's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Rothschild became chairman of the New York Urban Coalition from 1970 to 1973, then served as chairman of the National Urban Coalition from 1973 to 1977. He also sat on the state commission that investigated the Attica prison r
2 CONC iots.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was instrumental in the 1979 merger of St. Luke's Hospital and the Roosevelt Hospital Center and served as the combined entity's chairman from 1981 to 1987.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also served two terms on the Harvard University Board of Overseers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born to Walter N. Rothschild and Carola Warburg Rothschild in 1920, Mr. Rothschild graduated cum laude from Harvard in 1942 before enlisting in the Army in World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides his son, of New Milford, Conn., Mr. Rothschild is survived by his wife, Virginia, of Syosset, N.Y.; three daughters, Nina Rothschild-Utne of Minneapolis, Suzanne, of Arlington, Mass., and Jane, of Sydney, Australia; two si
2 CONC sters, Carol Noyes of Syosset and Phyllis Farley of Manhattan; and 10 grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution announces with great sorrow the death June 20, 2003, of Honorary Member of the Corporation Walter N. "Bill" Rothschild, Jr. at Huntington Hospital on Long Island of complications from surge
2 CONC ry. He was 83.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rothschild was a long time WHOI Associate, joining the program in the early 1970s. He was elected an Honorary Member of the Corporation in 1992.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in 1920, he graduated from Harvard in 1942 with a BA degree before enlisting in the U.S Army in World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT The great grandson of a founder of the department store chain Abraham & Straus, Walter Rothschild was the fourth generation of his family to serve as the company's president. He stepped down as President of Abraham & Straus in 196
2 CONC 9 after nine years to pursue a civic career. He served as chairman of the New York Urban Coalition from 1970 to 1973, and as chairman of the National Urban Coalition from 1973 to 1977. He also served on the New York state commissi
2 CONC on that investigated the riots at Attica prison.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Rothschild, Jr. was a Trustee of St. Luke's/Roosevelt Hospital Center and an Honorary Trustee of the Josiah Macy. Jr. Foundation and the US Trust Company of New York. He served as a director of the Jacob K. Javits Conventio
2 CONC n Center's Operating Corporation, was a member of the Advisory Committee on the Arts at Harvard and Radcliffe Colleges, and served on The President's Council of the United Hospital Fund.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by his wife, Virginia Rothschild, of Syosset, NY; one son, Trip, of Newfield, CT; three daughters, Nina Rothschild-Utne of Minneapolis, MN, Suzanne Rothschild of Arlington, MA, and Jane Rothschild of Sydney, Austral
2 CONC ia; two sisters, Carol Noyes of Syosset, NY and Phyllis Farley or Manhattan; and 10 grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT A funeral will be held Tuesday, June 24, at 11:30 a.m. at Temple Emanuel, 65th Street and 5th Avenue, in New York City. A reception will follow at the Cosmopolitan Club, 122 East 66th Street.
