1 OCCU Physical scientist; Director Imperial Astrophysical Observatory in Potsdam
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Schwarzschild '''Karl Schwarzschild'''] (October 9, 1873 – May 11, 1916) was a German physicist and astronomer. He was also the father of astrophysicist Martin Schwarzschild.
2 CONT
2 CONT Schwarzschild provided the first exact solution to the Einstein field equations of general relativity, for the limited case of a single spherical non-rotating mass, which he accomplished in 1915, the same year that Einstein first 
2 CONC introduced general relativity. The Schwarzschild solution, which makes use of Schwarzschild coordinates and the Schwarzschild metric, leads to a derivation of the Schwarzschild radius, which is the size of the event horizon of a n
2 CONC on-rotating black hole.
2 CONT
2 CONT Schwarzschild accomplished this while serving in the German army during World War I. He died the following year from the autoimmune disease pemphigus, which he developed while at the Russian front). Various forms of the disease pa
2 CONC rticularly affect people of Ashkenazi Jewish origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Asteroid 837 Schwarzschilda is named in his honour, as is the large crater Schwarzschild, on the far side of the moon.
2 CONT
2 CONT * From https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karl_Schwarzschild, accessed 7 February 2019.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography of Karl Schwarzschild (1873–1916)
2 CONT Karl Schwarzschild. Courtesy AIP Emilio Segrè Visual Archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT The celebrated German astronomer and physicist, Karl Schwarzschild, was born into a wealthy Jewish family in Frankfurt am Main, Germany, on October 9, 1873. He was the oldest of his five brothers and one sister. His mother, Henrie
2 CONC tta Sabel, and father, Moses Martin Schwarzschild, were very nice and hearthy persons. His large, extended family was known to cultivate deep interest in art and culture. However, he became the first in the family to pursue a care
2 CONC er in science.
2 CONT
2 CONT Schwarzschild’s prodigious talent manifested quite early on while still a student at Frankfurt Gymnasium: at the age of 16, having taught himself some advanced mathematics and studied much of contemporary astronomy, he published h
2 CONC is first scientific paper on celestial mechanics.
2 CONT
2 CONT This was soon followed by another paper. Both papers touched upon the orbital theory of binary stars. In his Gymnasium years, he was a close friend of the famous mathematician specializing in number theory, Paul Epstein, with whom
2 CONC  there was a sharing of real scientific interests.
2 CONT
2 CONT He further studied astronomy at the Universities of Strasbourg and Munich, obtaining his doctorate in 1896 for a dissertation on the application of Poincaré’s theory of rotating stellar bodies. His supervisor was Hugo von Seeliger
2 CONC  whom he often mentioned with much admiration throughout his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1897 until 1899, he was employed as an assistant at the Kuffner Observatory in the suburb of Vienna called Ottakring. Here, he was en- gaged in the investigation and measurement of the apparent brightness of stars using photo
2 CONC graphic plates, from which he produced a formula to calculate the optical density of photographic material. This formula was especially important in dealing with photographic measurements of the intensities of distant, faint astro
2 CONC nomical objects.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1899, he became a privatdozent at the University of Munich after submitting his habilitation thesis entitled ''Beiträge zur photographischen Photometrie der Gestirne'' which dealt with much of the astronomical wor
2 CONC k he had done at the Kuffner Observatory.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is particularly interesting to note that already in 1900, Schwarzschild already pondered upon the possible non-Euclidean structure of space. His ideas were expounded at the meeting of the German Astronomical Society in Heidelbe
2 CONC rg that year. In the same year, he published a paper in which he gave a lower limit for the (measurable) radius of the curvature of space as 64 light years (supposing a hyperbolic space) or 1600 light years (an elliptic space). In
2 CONC  dealing with solar radiation pressure, he assumed that the tails of comets consisted of spherical particles which acted as light reflectors. Thus he was able to calculate the size of the particles in the tails of the comets. He i
2 CONC nstinctively knew that radiation pressure had to somehow overcome gravitation, and that the particles did not scatter light. This way, he gave the exact diameters of the particles within the range of 0.07 and 1.5 microns. From 190
2 CONC 1 until 1909 he was an extraordinary professor at the University of Göttingen and also the director of the Observatory there. In Göttingen, he had the opportunity to work with some significant figures inhabiting the University, su
2 CONC ch as the mathematicians David Hilbert, Felix Klein, and Hermann Minkowski. He studied astrophys- ical phenomena associated with the energy transport mechanism in a star by means of radiation and produced an important paper on rad
2 CONC iative equilibrium within the sun’s atmosphere. Following this period, he took up a post of the director at the Astronomical Observatory in Pots- dam in 1909, a place which Eddington described for him as “...very congenial. . . ” 
2 CONC [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913, Schwarzschild was elected a member of the Prussian Academy of Sciences in Berlin. During his election, he produced a memorable speech which outlined the essence of his attitude towards science [1]:
2 CONT
2 CONT >> “Mathematics, physics, chemistry, astronomy, march in one front. Whichever lags behind is drawn after. Whichever hastens ahead helps on the others. The closest solidarity between astronomy and the whole circle of exact science.
2 CONC  . . . from this aspect I may count it well that my interest has never been limited to the things beyond the moon, but has followed the threads which spin themselves from there to our sublunar knowledge; I have often been untrue t
2 CONC o the heavens. That is an impulse to the universal which was strengthened unwittingly by my teacher Seeliger, and afterwards was further nourished by Felix Klein and the whole scientific circle at Göttingen. There the motto runs t
2 CONC hat mathematics, physics, and astronomy constitute one knowledge, which, like the Greek culture, is only comprehended as a perfect whole.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon, in 1914, Europe was plagued with the outbreak of World War I. Subsequently, he joined the German army as a volunteer despite being over 40 years old. He served on both the Eastern and Western Fronts, eventually rising to the
2 CONC  rank of lieutenant in the artillery division. Notably, he served in Belgium where he was put in charge of a local weather station, in France where he produced calculations of missile trajectories, and then in Russia.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Russia, despite suffering from a rare and painful skin dis- ease called pemphigus, he managed to write three pivotal papers: two on the exact solutions to Einstein’s field equations of the General Theory of Relativity, th
2 CONC e new theory of space-time and gravitation, and one on Planck’s quantum theory. As it is well-known, his papers on the General Theory of Relativity gave the first exact solutions to Einstein’s unimodular field equations of gravita
2 CONC tion in the empty space surrounding a spherical mass, a solution which now bears his name, the Schwarzschild metric, which actually involves a slight modification of his original solution. Meanwhile, his paper on quantum theory ga
2 CONC ve a lucid explanation of the so-called Stark effect.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon receiving Schwarzschild’s manuscripts, Einstein himself was pleasantly surprised to learn that his non-linear field equations of gravitation did admit exact solutions, despite their “prima facie” complexity, which, according 
2 CONC to him, were elegantly shown by Schwarzschild in “. . . such a simple way. . . ” [2]. Prior to this, Einstein himself was only able to produce an approximate solution, given in his famous 1915 pa- per on the advance of the perihel
2 CONC ion of Mercury. In that paper, Einstein employed a rectilinear coordinate system in order to approximate the gravitational field around a spherically symmetric, static, non-rotating, non-charged mass. Schwarzschild, in contrast to
2 CONC  Einstein’s initial approach, chose a generalization of the polar coordinate system and was thus able to produce an exact solution in a more elegant manner, a manner somewhat more befitting the splendour and subtlety of the full n
2 CONC on- Euclidean nature of Einstein’s geometric theory.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1916, the elated Einstein famously wrote to Schwarzschild on his newly obtained result [2]:
2 CONT
2 CONT >> “I have read your paper with the utmost interest. I had not expected that one could formulate the exact solution of the problem in such a simple way. I liked very much your mathematical treatment of the subject. Next Thursday I
2 CONC  shall present the work to the Academy with a few words of explanation.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after he sent his last two papers on the General Theory of Relativity to Einstein, Schwarzschild had to succumb to the skin dis- ease he had contracted earlier. The disease, pemphigus, is a rare kind of autimmune blisterin
2 CONC g skin rash. It is said that for people plagued with this skin rash, the immune system mistakes the cells in the skin as foreign and attacks them, resulting in painful blisters. In Schwarzschild’s time there was no known medical t
2 CONC reatment or cure for the disease and, after being freed from his military duty to be interned at home in March 1916, he died two months later, on May 11, 1916, at the age of 42.
2 CONT
2 CONT Schwarzschild died at the height of his scientific achievements. He certainly was a man of wide scientific interests. Apart from his earlier work on astronomy, which included celestial mechanics, observational stellar photometry, 
2 CONC optical systems, observational and instrumental astronomy, stellar structure and statistics, comets, and spectroscopy, and his outstanding work in the area of the General Theory of Relativity, he also worked on electrodynamics and
2 CONC  geometrical optics (while in Göttingen). He also maintained deep interest in quantum theory.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Else Posenbach, the daughter of a professor of surgery at the University of Göttingen, on 22 October 1909. Together, they had three children: Agathe, Martin, and Alfred. His second son, Mar- tin Schwarzschild, followed 
2 CONC in his father’s footsteps as a professor of astrophysics at Princeton University.
2 CONT
2 CONT Indranu Suhendro
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Eddington A. S. Karl Schwarzschild. Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society, 1917, vol. 77, 314–319.
2 CONT 2. Eisenstaedt J. The early interpretation of the Schwarzschild solution. In: Einstein and the History of General Relativity: Proceedings, 1986 Osgood Hill Conference (Einstein Studies, vol.1), ed. by D.Howard and J.Stachel, Birkh
2 CONC auser, Boston, 1989, 213–233.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vol. 1, 2008	ISSN 1654-9163 THE ABRAHAM ZELMANOV JOURNAL The journal for General Relativity, gravitation and cosmology TIDSKRIFTEN ABRAHAM ZELMANOV Den tidskrift f ̈or allm ̈anna relativitetsteorin, gravitation och kosmologi Edito
2 CONC r (redakt ̈or):	Dmitri Rabounski Secretary (sekreterare):	Indranu Suhendro The Abraham Zelmanov Journal is a non-commercial, academic journal registered with the Royal National Library of Sweden. This journal was typeset using LAT
2 CONC EX typesetting system. Powered by Ubuntu Linux.
2 CONT
