1 NOTE {geni:about_me}   DuBois Daily Express, Friday, October 9, 1914
2 CONT Sudden Death of Well Known Citizen of DuBois
2 CONT Sylvester Goldsmith, a well known business man of this place, died quite suddenly last evening at his home on Park avenue of neuralgia of the heart. He had been ailing for several days, but had been up and around the house. On Tue
2 CONC sday he was down town attending to business and last evening he was on his front porch when the band passed out Brady street on the way to the tabernacle. A little later he entered the house and at 8:45 o'clock suddenly expired wh
2 CONC ile sitting on a rocking chair.
2 CONT The deceased was born in Philadelphia and had he lived until November 4th next, he would have been 45 years of age. He was the son of Levi and Henrietta Goldsmith, both now deceased. For many years he was in business in Indianapol
2 CONC is, Ind., but thirteen years ago he came to DuBois and has resided here ever since. He was at one time engaged in the clothing business with Warren Baxter, near the B., R. & P. station. Later he was associated with the Meads in th
2 CONC e pool room business and about two years ago he engaged in business for himself, opening a pool room, opposite the Martin Brothers store on North Brady street.
2 CONT Socially Mr. Goldsmith was a member of the Knights of Pythias and the Loyal Order of the Moose. He leaves to survive him his wife, whose maiden name was Miss Ida Simms, and who was from Lima, Ohio, and five children as follows: Lo
2 CONC uis, Harold, Blanchard, Estelle and Francis. He also leaves three sisters and one brother, namely, Mrs. Harry Loeb, Mrs. D. Greenbaum, Mrs. Athens and George Goldsmith, all of Philadelphia.
2 CONT Funeral services will be held Sunday afternoon at 2 o'clock and interment will be made in the Rumbarger cemetery.
